WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.910
- [Daniela] In response
to the coronavirus crisis,

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.210
researchers are working
at a breakneck pace

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:07.730
to develop solutions
that will allow economies

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:09.960
to reopen safely and permanently.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.590
- Without an effective vaccine,

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:16.100
we won't be able to get
this pandemic under control

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.770
and stop the devastation
that it's currently causing

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:20.310
all around the globe.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:21.870
- Vaccines can take an average

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:25.810
of 10 years to develop,
according to a 2013 study,

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:28.060
but researchers and
pharmaceutical companies

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.220
are hoping to bring
their vaccine candidates

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:33.990
for the new coronavirus to
market in 12 to 18 months.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:35.650
Some are even talking about getting

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:39.010
Emergency Use Authorizations
before the end of the year.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:40.720
So what is making it possible

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.570
to expedite this process so dramatically?

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:45.050
There are several factors,

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:47.770
including prior knowledge
of coronaviruses,

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:50.030
specifically SARS and MERS,

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.870
improvements in sequencing
of viral genomes,

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:55.750
advancements in
bioengineering technologies,

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:58.390
unprecedented government
support and funding,

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:00.840
plus a shortened testing timeline.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:04.340
Traditionally, after scientists
develop a vaccine candidate,

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:06.470
they run it through a series of tests.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:09.730
This starts in cells, and
then transitions to animals,

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:11.530
usually beginning with mice

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:14.280
and eventually progressing to monkeys.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.440
Testing only advances
from one phase to the next

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:20.220
if it's proven to be safe and effective.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.530
- At that stage, you
would then likely perform

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.430
what we call a challenge study,

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:27.110
so that would be vaccinating an animal

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:29.550
then intentionally giving them the virus

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:33.590
and seeing the impact of your
vaccine on that disease model

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.620
where, of course, you
would hope to protect it.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.680
- Then they'd use that data to
get approval from regulators

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.440
like the FDA to begin human testing.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:43.930
There are three stages of human trials,

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:46.710
each one testing more
people than the last.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:49.830
Approval is required for each stage.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:51.510
Instead of this relay approach

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:54.170
where researchers wrap up
each set of experiments

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:56.170
before embarking on the next round,

00:01:56.170 --> 00:02:00.070
developers are now doing
many tests in parallel.

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:03.720
- So we're not skipping
steps, but we're doing things

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:06.875
at the same time, which
of course costs a lot more

00:02:06.875 --> 00:02:10.503
and is a lot more work than
doing it one after the other,

00:02:10.503 --> 00:02:13.140
and that's the key difference.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.960
- The novel coronavirus was
discovered and sequenced

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:17.720
less than six months ago.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.170
Several candidates are already
in phase one of human trials,

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:23.810
including those made by Inovio and Pfizer,

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:27.080
even as companies await
results from animal studies.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.270
Moderna recently started phase two.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:30.960
Others are gearing up for phase two

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.400
before phase one wraps up.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.540
- Our phase one trial will run for a year,

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.360
but we're not going to wait
for that full year period

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:40.870
to have elapsed before
we hopefully move on

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:43.120
to the next stage of efficacy testing.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:47.563
- Because of the worry of
COVID-19 being so devastating,

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.290
we are expediting those processes,

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:54.040
and this oughta concern,
because we don't really know

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.600
all that much about
immunity to this virus,

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.910
that we may be going too quickly,

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:00.800
but so that's the balance here.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.070
- [Daniela] Vaccines
work on a simple premise.

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:06.530
They trick the body into thinking
a virus has infiltrated it

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:08.640
so the immune system kicks into action

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.470
and starts producing
antibodies and other defenses

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:13.290
against parts of the virus.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.770
In this case, scientists
think their target

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:18.770
is the spike protein,
which the coronavirus uses

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:20.350
to get into cells.

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:23.390
- It's been shown from SARS and MERS

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:26.200
that the spike protein
is the major protein

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:29.547
on the outside of the virus
of these coronaviruses

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:33.970
to which erase neutralizing antibodies,

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:37.950
and neutralizing
antibodies is what you need

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:39.600
to fight the infection.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.620
- So what would really end the pandemic

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:46.270
is when there's enough
immunity in the population,

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:47.520
and either than immunity

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:51.620
comes from everybody getting infected

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:53.860
or it comes from a vaccine,

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:57.980
and it's a much gentler path
for everybody to get a vaccine

00:03:57.980 --> 00:03:59.920
than for everybody to get infected.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.730
- [Daniela] This concept
is known as herd immunity.

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:06.410
Dozens of companies and labs
are trying various technologies

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:09.350
to develop vaccines that
would get us there quickly.

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:11.230
Scientists say it's the largest,

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:13.360
most collaborative
vaccine development effort

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.750
they've seen in their lifetimes.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.240
They're testing four main strategies,

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.520
weakened viruses, proteins,

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:23.590
viral vectors, and genetic code vaccines.

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:26.040
Weakened or inactivated virus vaccines

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:27.340
are the most common.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:29.830
That's your annual flu shot.

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:31.880
We have the most experience with these,

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:33.350
but they can also be costly

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:35.510
and time-consuming to mass-produce,

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.410
so in this case, they haven't
been the preferred approach.

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:40.690
Protein-based vaccines

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:43.340
carry key parts of the
virus into the body.

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:46.020
For coronavirus, that's the spike protein.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:48.180
Sanofi and GlaxoSmithKline

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:51.230
are among dozens of developers
taking this approach.

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:52.850
For viral vector vaccines,

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:55.780
scientists use an inactivated
virus and stuff it

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:57.910
with the genetic code
for the spike protein,

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:00.880
which our cells then use
to turn out viral proteins.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.100
The immune system then
recognizes those as foreign.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.900
Johnson &amp; Johnson is
developing this type of vaccine

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:08.860
for the coronavirus.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:12.090
- [Gert] We use adenovirus vector,

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:14.900
and this adenovirus is a common cold virus

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:18.200
which normally doesn't
cause any major disease,

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:19.890
just a common cold.

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.993
In the adenovirus, it becomes
replication incompetent.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:26.760
- [Daniela] In the
fourth and final category

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:28.580
are genetic code vaccines.

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:31.770
These contain the instructions
for the spike protein.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:34.350
COVID vaccines being developed by Inovio,

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:37.500
Pfizer, and Moderna
fall into this category.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:39.730
These types of vaccines
have never been approved

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:41.060
for humans before,

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:43.540
but they're generating
a lot of excitement.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:46.493
- The reason we're seeing them
out in front is the speed.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:50.160
- The speed in the manufacturing
and the development?

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.020
- Yeah, really, both.

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:53.240
Proteins are more diverse.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:54.810
They have all kinds of different shapes

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:59.460
and characteristics and so you
have to play with a lot more

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:03.210
to actually get a
protein-based vaccine made.

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.200
- [Daniela] Pfizer is
running multiple candidates

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:07.760
through phase one trials
in the U.S. and Germany

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:10.210
to cut down on development time.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:13.960
- And even before we know
whether the vaccine works or not,

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:16.590
we're starting to ramp
up manufacturing capacity

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:18.730
so that we're ready to make it right away.

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:24.500
The key things you have to
establish to develop a vaccine

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:27.760
are that it is safe, that it's effective,

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:30.320
and that every time you make
it, it comes out the same,

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:34.330
so those key steps don't change
whether you're going fast

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:35.640
or you're going slow.

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:38.770
- Changes in manufacturing can
introduce unforeseen issues

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:40.950
that could make a vaccine dangerous.

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:43.250
That's what happened with
the since-discontinued

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:45.560
polio vaccine decades ago.

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:47.250
Safety is a major concern,

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:49.800
because vaccines are
given to healthy people,

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:52.260
so scientists have to ensure
they don't affect things

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:55.343
like liver or heart function
among other factors.

00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:58.410
There are other issues as well.

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:01.230
Even if companies are able
to come up with a vaccine

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:04.520
in less than two years, the
vaccine research community

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:06.210
is concerned with how they'd be able

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:09.820
to produce and distribute it
to everybody on the planet.

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:12.910
- So we are aiming to
produce one billion dose

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:16.760
by the end of 2021, and
some other companies

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:20.030
are committing to the same kind of scale,

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:22.130
either hundreds of millions or billions,

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:24.200
but still, it will not
be enough for everyone.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:27.350
- What is a big unknown
is what the durability

00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:28.410
of that protection is.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:32.130
Is it going to be a year, two years,

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:35.920
or even maybe, unfortunately,
six months or less,

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:39.720
because if it is measured
in several months

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:43.000
instead of years, which
is entirely conceivable

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.640
that that would be the case,

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:46.830
then we have a secondary problem.

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:49.270
How often do you need to boost somebody

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:51.890
to keep that level of immunity up?

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:54.460
- [Daniela] The final and
perhaps most important hurdle

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:58.003
for coronavirus vaccine
development will be human behavior.

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:01.250
- Are people gonna be
accepting of this vaccine?

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:04.771
That's been such a huge
problem in this country.

00:08:04.771 --> 00:08:06.290
You know, if you have 50% of people

00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:08.430
saying I'm not gonna take
it under any condition,

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:10.653
then, of course, you've
lost the whole battle.

